  "terminal_reason": "completed",
  "final_text": "I've built the dashboard and the alert rule in Kibana, and the rule is enabled. Its first run came back with status `ok`. The rule's 5xx query matches 441 log entries, which is every 503 in the data.\n\n**1. What the data holds.** `kibana_sample_data_logs` has about 14,000 web requests with timestamps from 4 Oct to 3 Dec 2026. Each request records:\n- **Response codes:** only three appear \u2014 200 (12,832), 404 (801) and 503 (441). Every 503 response has 0 bytes.\n- **Request details:** bytes, URL, file extension and referer.\n- **Client details:** client IP, source and destination country, operating system and user agent.\n\n**2. Dashboard \"EFF-313034b2 Web logs overview\"** ([open](http://localhost:15601/app/dashboards#/view/eff-313034b2-web-logs-overview-33e695c55345)) has seven panels:\n- Three single numbers: total requests, unique visitors (distinct client IPs) and total bytes served.\n- Requests over time, split by response code.\n- A pie chart of response codes.\n- The top 10 URLs, with request count and bytes.\n- The top 10 source countries.\n\nI set the default time range to run from 7 days ago to 60 days ahead, because much of the sample data is dated in the future.\n\n**3. Rule \"EFF-313034b2 5xx spike\"** (id `794d1813-87c0-437a-b952-bd81577fb003`) runs every minute. It counts requests in `kibana_sample_data_logs` whose response code is between 500 and 599, using the `timestamp` field. It fires when that count is more than 10 within 5 minutes.\n\nThe rule has no action connector, so when it fires the alert shows up in Kibana but no notification is sent anywhere. If you want email, Slack or something similar, a connector needs to be added.",
